00:00:15.811 --> 00:00:18.436
We don’t know how many rites of
passage there are

00:00:18.436 --> 00:00:21.206
institutionalized among 
amateur microscopists.

00:00:21.206 --> 00:00:25.330
But we do have to imagine, that as people find
themselves navigating the microcosmos for

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.859
the first time, they’re often on the lookout
for tardigrades.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:36.469
Their funny appearance and legendary resistance
have made tardigrades icons of the microscopic

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.345
world, and who doesn’t love an icon?

00:00:39.345 --> 00:00:46.120
Recently, our master of microscopes James
decided to try out a new way to gather tardigrades.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.665
The method is simple, and the results quite
astounding.

00:00:50.665 --> 00:00:55.195
All you  need is some mesh, water, and
a little bit of patience.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.419
James has a little patch of moss next to his
trash bins.

00:01:11.419 --> 00:01:18.000
So when he takes his trash out, he’ll sometimes
collect bits of the moss to look for microbes later.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.030
And we have seen tardigrades from 
these bits of moss before.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:27.579
The question is, though, how to easily and
efficiently get the tardigrades to emerge

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:31.720
from the moss so that James can watch them
under the microscope.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.720
Recently, James was inspired to try out something
called the Baermann funnel, which he’d found

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:37.720
in a book.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.750
This technique goes back to at least 1917,
when it was first described by a Dutch physician

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:47.398
named Gustav Karl Theodor Friedrich Baermann.

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:52.909
He invented the method when he was in Java,
looking for nematodes that live in soils—

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:55.874
particularly parasitic hookworms.

00:01:55.874 --> 00:02:01.100
And since then, the tool has become one of
several that nematode researchers use to gather

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:07.380
their research subjects, in part because it is
just so easy to set up.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.190
Here we can see just how simple James’ version
is.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:16.914
To make one of your own, start by placing
some soil or moss on a mesh fabric.

00:02:16.914 --> 00:02:20.910
There’s a number of things you can use for
the mesh, including tea bags, a pair of

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.315
tights, or a cheesecloth.

00:02:23.315 --> 00:02:29.760
Then place the mesh/moss combination into
a funnel and carefully add water until only

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.290
just part of the moss is dipped in the water.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.220
Make sure to seal off the bottom of the funnel—

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.786
in James’ case, he used a piece of rubber
tube.

00:02:39.786 --> 00:02:45.909
The next day, you can return to see what organisms
have gone through the mesh and are now settled

00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:47.900
at the bottom of the funnel.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.239
The Baermann funnel has proven to be a lasting
tool for nematologists to find worms buried

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.500
in soil.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.720
They begin with the soil sample above the
mesh, and with the addition of water, the

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.225
worms are released from the soil.

00:03:01.225 --> 00:03:06.099
This sets them free to travel to the mesh
and then through it, ultimately settling at

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.456
the bottom of the funnel.

00:03:07.456 --> 00:03:13.980
And after anywhere from 16-72 hours, the nematologist
can return to find a bundle of microscopic

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.159
worms at the bottom.

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:19.197
But this doesn’t necessarily work for all
nematodes.

00:03:19.197 --> 00:03:24.500
Nematodes that live in plants can take longer
to come out, while other species will travel

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.637
away from the bottom of the funnel.

00:03:26.637 --> 00:03:31.720
So when James decided to try this technique
out to find tardigrades, there was no guarantee

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.310
that it would actually work.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:39.700
He returned the day after setting up his tardigrade-trapping
apparatus and had to just hope for the best.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.429
He collected a drop of water from the bottom
of the funnel and prepared a slide.

00:03:44.429 --> 00:03:47.825
What he saw was incredible.

00:03:47.825 --> 00:03:55.450
He counted 196 tardigrades on his very first
slide.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.000
That is a lot of tardigrades!

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.910
If you try the Baermann funnel out and find
that your tardigrades are a bit hard to see

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:08.099
because bits of sediment or other things
floating in your sample, don’t worry, James

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:09.846
had this same issue too.

00:04:09.846 --> 00:04:15.000
They’re kind of hard to make out here because
there’s just so much stuff for them to hide in.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.930
He just washed the tardigrades a bit to make
them a little more presentable for us, carefully

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:25.877
pipetting drops of water until there was minimal
debris to obscure our view.

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:30.430
And one of the cool things about having caught
so many tardigrades at once is that you can

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.787
see how much variety there is to them.

00:04:33.787 --> 00:04:39.190
Like here we have a few tardigrades that look
like giants crawling in a variety pack of

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.140
miniature tardigrades.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.580
You can also catch tardigrades in some funny
situations, like this tardigrade that’s

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.346
using its clawed feet to walk along the glass
slide.

00:04:49.346 --> 00:04:55.017
Tardigrades cannot swim, but they will use
their claws to walk around underwater.

00:04:55.017 --> 00:05:00.677
And glass is a tough surface for this one
at the moment, but over time, it will get better—

00:05:00.677 --> 00:05:03.918
though not because of anything the glass
or tardigrade does.

00:05:03.918 --> 00:05:09.800
Over time, those little dots of bacteria you
see across the screen will accumulate.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.289
And as the number of bacterias grow and spread
across the glass, their bodies will create

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:20.240
friction along the surface, which means the
tardigrade will be better equipped to walk

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.389
across it.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:26.157
James also found this tardigrade who looks
trapped in a bag.

00:05:26.157 --> 00:05:32.550
In reality, she’s stuck to her cuticle,
which is full of eggs that will one day hatch

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.312
and replenish the world of tardigrades.

00:05:35.312 --> 00:05:41.039
So the Baermann funnel helped James find a
ton of tardigrades without much fuss.

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:45.940
But there’s one major thing he did have
to keep in mind: oxygen.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:50.370
When he first added the moss to the funnel,
the tardigrades were in their resting tun

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:56.652
state, their entire being temporarily on pause
so they could survive the desiccated conditions.

00:05:56.652 --> 00:06:01.630
And when James added water and created more
favorable conditions, the tardigrades emerged

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.470
from the tuns and began walking around.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:09.180
So ideally, you will give the tardigrades
enough time to unfreeze themselves.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:15.000
But if you wait too long, then the water in
the funnel begins to form an oxygen gradient.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.472
The surface will have more oxygen, while the
bottom will have less. 

00:06:19.472 --> 00:06:24.090
And that’s a problem because that bottom
region is where the tardigrades end up

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.600
settling as we collect them through the mesh.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.228
As more and more tardigrades 
accumulate in that bottom area,

00:06:31.228 --> 00:06:33.517
they will have to compete 
with each other for oxygen.

00:06:33.517 --> 00:06:39.630
And sadly, the oxygen concentration can change
very rapidly, killing the tardigrades before

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.652
they can even return to their protective tuns.

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:45.840
But there are a few ways to prevent the death
of your tardigrades.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.424
Try and collect the tardigrades from the funnel
sooner rather than later.

00:06:49.424 --> 00:06:53.780
It may take some trial and error to find the
right timing, but James says to try and avoid

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.807
waiting longer than a day.

00:06:55.807 --> 00:07:00.180
And when James does collect his tardigrades,
he provides them with fresh water to replace

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.220
the oxygen-deficient 
water they're living in.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:08.100
He also adds some algae from his pond samples
into the slides and keeps them in transparent

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.720
humidity chambers under a light source.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.723
The light helps the algae reproduce, providing 
 tardigrades with both food and oxygen.

00:07:19.097 --> 00:07:23.460
These are the refinements that James has made
to the Baermann funnel to make it work for

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.880
his tardigrades.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.150
And depending on what materials you have available
to you and the samples you’re collecting,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.789
you might have to make your own creative adaptations.

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:39.530
The joy of the microcosmos is that it is
all around us, but it can also take time and

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:44.682
practice to figure out the best ways to actually
find its inhabitants.

00:07:44.682 --> 00:07:48.430
So we hope this technique helps you all on
your own journey.

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:53.121
And if you try the Baermann funnel out on
your hunt for nematodes and tardigrades, make

00:07:53.121 --> 00:07:55.898
sure to share what you find with us.

